Common ETL Pattern

Every ingestion script follows the same production pattern.

Configuration

↓

Validate File

↓

Read CSV

↓

Validate Columns

↓

Generate Metadata

↓

Convert Data

↓

Bulk Insert

↓

Success Message

This made me realize that production pipelines are designed to be reusable and consistent.

Defensive Programming

Before reading the CSV, the pipeline first validates that the file exists.

Instead of allowing multiple downstream errors, the pipeline immediately stops.

One lesson I learned:

The earlier I detect the problem, the easier it is to debug.

Configuration

Instead of hardcoding values throughout the script, all configurable values are placed at the top.

Examples:

CSV Path
Database Configuration
Source File
Batch ID

This makes the pipeline easier to maintain.

Bulk Loading

Instead of inserting one row at a time, I prepared the records first before sending them to PostgreSQL.

This approach is cleaner and much closer to how production pipelines handle large datasets.

